      "title": "The Tree and the River: A Wordless Love Letter to How Nature Always Wins",
      "content_html": "Nested between two giant cedars in the remote old-growth forest where I live much of the year are the rusted remains of boxy car. A tree root has seized one of the rear wheels. A sapling has growth through the glassless windshield. Bright orange fungi have colonized the driver&#8217;s seat. Lichen and moss cover nearly every inch of the chipped blue body. It is a living, wildly alive metaphor for the fate of every civilization, for the sinuous tenacity by which nature always prevails. That is what Aaron Becker celebrates in his wordless wonder The Tree and the River (public&#8230;&#160;<a class=\"h3 read_on\" href=\"https://www.themarginalian.org/2026/08/28/tree-river-becker/\">read&#160;article</a>",
